    "notes": "Philip the Good presided over the Burgundian state at its apogee, assembling the Low Countries and much of northern France into a composite dominion of unrivalled wealth and cultural splendour. Through the Order of the Golden Fleece and patronage of artists such as Jan van Eyck he made his court a model of princely magnificence, while the Treaty of Arras (1435) reoriented Burgundy toward France. His realm remained a personal union of disparate territories rather than a consolidated state, a fragility exposed after his death.",
